messages:
vikram: Quick Q — SDKs are getting 429s right after a per-route quota resets. The bucket shows room over the window but clients still hit 429s for short, sparse bursts. Is this expected? Any recommended client-side warmup or backoff guidance? Hosted vs Dedicated differences?

emily: short answer: expected in some cases. If traffic is very sparse, the runtime's burst shaping + sliding-window accounting can still produce temporary 429s because of how we bucket recent tokens. Also cold KV cache + long prompts amplify token counts.

dan: adding detail — we use a leaky/sliding-window for per-route quotas. Internally we permit short bursts up to a configured burst_capacity but that interacts with other signals (concurrent inflight, per-instance throttles). On Hosted you'll see more conservative trimming during global contention; Dedicated tends to be more permissive but still respects the logical per-route quota.

kate: practical guidance for SDKs:
- avoid fan-out: don’t send many parallel requests that expand the same logical user prompt.
- warmup ramp: for new routes or after reset, ramp requests over ~30–90s instead of firing full QPS immediately.
- prefetch tiny probe: send a small 1–2 token request to populate caches (if you rely on server-side caching) before large requests.
- backoff: exponential backoff + full jitter, start at 100–200ms.

vikram: nice. For headers, should SDKs rely on Retry-After vs our X-RateLimit headers? We have clients in the wild that only look for Retry-After and then hammer again.

emily: prefer X-Quota-Remaining / X-Quota-Window-Reset for quota-aware clients. Retry-After is present on hard 429s but can be coarse. Suggested header set:
- X-Quota-Remaining: tokens left for this window
- X-Quota-Window-Reset: epoch sec when window slides
- Retry-After: seconds until safe retry (on severe throttles)

dan: also plan to add X-Burst-Available: small int indicating short-burst-credits remaining (helps SDKs decide immediate retries). We'll add it to the contract but keep behavior conservative for backward-compat.

kate: sample client pattern we recommend (pseudocode):
```js
async function sendWithWarmup(req) {
  if (firstRequestAfterReset()) await sleep(200) // gentle ramp
  try {
    return await api.send(req)
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.status === 429) {
      const ra = parseInt(err.headers['retry-after'] || '0')
      const remaining = parseInt(err.headers['x-quota-remaining'] || '-1')
      const burst = parseInt(err.headers['x-burst-available'] || '0')
      // backoff with jitter
      const backoff = Math.max(ra * 1000, base * 2 ** attempts) * Math.random()
      await sleep(backoff)
      return sendWithWarmup(req)
    }
    throw err
  }
}
```

ops-bot: FYI metrics dashboard: https://dash.redwood.internal/quotas/route-summary?route=/v1/generate — shows recent sparse-burst spikes (cold-cache)

vikram: two quick follow-ups:
1) Can we document X-Burst-Available semantics and a fallback when header absent? e.g., treat absence as 0 and use Retry-After.
2) Any objections to adding a one-time SDK opt-in that does a 200ms probe when route version changes (to smooth rollout-induced spikes)?

dan: +1 for both. For opt-in probe, make it opt-in default-off for Hosted; default-on for Dedicated in the internal SDK builds.

emily: small note on rollout/backcompat: header additions are additive. Clients should tolerate missing fields. We'll add a deprecation/design note in the API contract doc.

kate: I'll draft an SDK guidance PR and ping here for a quick review. Will include examples for JavaScript, Python, and Go.

vikram: perfect, thanks all. @kate ping me when PR is up, will add sample tests for warmup behavior. :thumbsup:
